From: Ming-Ying Chung <mych@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:48:27 -0700
Subject: [FSA] Block macOS firmlink alias paths to sensitive directories

On macOS 10.15+, the system storage layout splits into a read-only
System volume mounted at "/" and a read-write Data volume mounted at
"/System/Volumes/Data". APFS firmlinks map directories such as /Users,
/Applications, and /Library between the two volumes without modifying
standard POSIX realpath representations.

Previously, ChromeFileSystemAccessPermissionContext matched candidate
file and directory paths lexically against canonical blocklist rules
registered under the "/" root namespace. When a target path was
referenced or resolved via "/System/Volumes/Data/...", component-wise
path matching diverged at the leading path component, failing open and
bypassing sensitive directory protections for targets like ~/.ssh,
~/Library, and Chrome User Data.

To prevent blocklist bypasses while preserving legitimate user access,
this change introduces MaybeStripMacDataVolumePrefix() on macOS to strip
the "/System/Volumes/Data" prefix and fold paths onto the "/" namespace
prior to ancestor evaluation in ShouldBlockAccessToPath(). Additionally,
it registers an explicit kBlockAllChildren rule for /System/Volumes in
GenerateBlockPaths() to block direct access to internal system container
volumes.

Bug: 562043997
Change-Id: Id80dfecafad651d28b691e10d960bc6e3ac5a763
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8410087
Reviewed-by: Mingyu Lei <leimy@chromium.org>
Commit-Queue: Ming-Ying Chung <mych@chromium.org>
